course: technology
lesson: 2
title: Chocolate and sweets
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Chocolate starts as bitter beans inside big pods on cocoa trees in hot countries, mostly in West Africa, and ends as a shiny bar in a lunchbox thousands of kilometres away.
explain: In between, machines roast and grind the beans, stir the paste with sugar and milk for hours, warm and cool it in a careful pattern, pour it into moulds and wrap the bars faster than you can count.
explain: Gummy bears are poured into shapes pressed in trays of cornflour, boiled sweets are really a kind of sugar glass, and shiny coloured sweets get their shells by tumbling in a drum for hours.
explain: If a bar left in a hot car melts and sets again, it turns grey and crumbly: the fat has set the wrong way, but it is still safe to eat.
explain: Melted sugar is far hotter than boiling water and sticks to skin, so in factories machines and gloved workers handle it, and children never boil sugar without an adult.
fact: Cocoa beans grow in pods on cocoa trees, mostly in West Africa, especially Ivory Coast and Ghana, and many are grown on small family farms.
fact: Many cocoa farmers earn only a small part of the price of a bar; fair-trade schemes try to pay them more, and people still argue about how well these schemes work.
fact: Farmers cut the pods, scoop out the beans, let them ferment (slowly change and turn a little sour, helped by tiny living things) under leaves for several days and dry them in the sun before they are shipped.
fact: Fresh cocoa beans taste bitter and nothing like chocolate; the chocolate taste comes from fermenting and roasting.
fact: In the factory the beans are roasted, ground into a paste, mixed with sugar and often milk, stirred for hours, tempered, poured into moulds, cooled and wrapped by machines.
fact: Tempering means warming and cooling melted chocolate in a careful pattern so the fat sets in neat tiny crystals that make the chocolate glossy and make it snap cleanly.
fact: Chocolate that melts and sets again often turns grey or white and crumbly because the fat crystals set the wrong way; it is still safe to eat.
fact: Filled bars are made by laying caramel or nougat as a long sheet, cutting it into bars and passing them on a wire belt through a falling curtain of melted chocolate, with a pool below for the bottoms; this is called enrobing.
fact: Gummy sweets are made by squirting hot syrup with gelatine or pectin into shapes pressed in trays of cornflour; the powder is sieved, dried and used again.
fact: A lollipop or boiled sweet is sugar syrup boiled until almost all the water is gone, so it sets hard, glassy and see-through, really a kind of sugar glass.
fact: Chewing gum is a rubbery gum base kneaded with sugar or sweetener and flavour; long ago people chewed a tree sap called chicle, and most gum base today is made in factories; spat gum on pavements costs towns a lot to clean.
fact: Shiny coloured sweets and sprinkles get their shells by panning: they tumble in big rotating drums while thin coats of sugar syrup and colour are sprayed on and dried, coat after coat, for hours.
fact: Wrapping machines wrap each sweet in film from a big roll and seal it with hot jaws, hundreds a minute, with guards in front and the machine stopped before any jam is cleared.
fact: A best-before date is about quality and a use-by date on fresh food is about safety; the batch number printed beside it lets a factory recall every packet from one bad batch.
exercise: Where do most cocoa beans grow? | In West Africa, especially Ivory Coast and Ghana | In Switzerland, right next to the big chocolate factories | In Belgium, on cocoa bushes in big fields | In northern Canada, in huge greenhouses
exercise: Who grows much of the world's cocoa? | Farmers, many of them on small family farms | Giant robots that work on huge cocoa plantations | Chocolate shops in big European cities like Paris | Nobody, cocoa only grows wild deep in the jungle
exercise: What do farmers do with cocoa beans before shipping them? | Let them ferment under leaves for days and dry them in the sun | Melt them into bars on the farm | Paint them brown so they look tasty | Freeze them straight from the tree
exercise: What do fresh cocoa beans taste like? | Bitter, nothing like chocolate | Sweet, just like milk chocolate | Salty, a bit like crisps | Sour, a bit like lemons
exercise: Where does the chocolate taste come from? | Fermenting and roasting the beans | Brown food colouring added at the end | The silver foil around the bar | Keeping the finished bar in the fridge
exercise: How do chocolate bars get their shape? | Melted chocolate is poured into moulds and cooled | Workers cut each bar from a giant block with knives | The cocoa beans grow in the shape of bars | Each bar is pressed out of chocolate powder
exercise: What is tempering? | Warming and cooling melted chocolate in a careful pattern | Adding a spoonful of salt to the chocolate | Freezing the chocolate as fast as possible so it stays hard | Shaking the bars in a tumbling drum
exercise: Why does good chocolate shine and snap cleanly? | Its fat set in neat tiny crystals during tempering | It is painted with a thin clear varnish before it is wrapped | It has crushed sugar glass mixed into it to make it crack | A machine polishes every single bar with a soft cloth
exercise: What is enrobing? | Passing bars through a curtain of melted chocolate to coat them | Wrapping each bar in silver foil and paper | Dipping the finished bars into cold water to set the chocolate quickly | Tying a ribbon round a box of sweets
exercise: How are gummy bears shaped? | Hot syrup is squirted into shapes pressed in trays of cornflour | Each bear is carved out of a big jelly block by hand with a knife | They are snipped from long jelly strings | They are stamped out of a flat jelly sheet by a robot
exercise: Why are boiled sweets hard and glassy? | Almost all the water was boiled out of the sugar syrup | They have tiny bits of real glass mixed into them for crunch | They are frozen for weeks until they are rock hard all through | They are baked in a hot oven for hours, just like bread
exercise: What did people chew as gum long ago? | A tree sap called chicle | Dried cocoa beans | Strips of old tyre rubber | Honey mixed with sand
exercise: How do sweets get a shiny coloured shell? | They tumble in drums while thin coats of syrup and colour are sprayed on | Each one is dipped by hand into paint | A laser prints the colour onto each sweet | The colour grows out from inside the sweet
exercise: How do wrapping machines seal sweets? | Hot jaws press the film shut | Workers lick and stick every wrapper | Tiny staples clip the two ends | Glue is brushed on by hand
exercise: What does a best-before date tell you? | Until when the food is at its best quality | The exact day the food turns poisonous to eat | The day the food was made in the factory itself | When the shop must throw the food away in the bin
exercise: What is the batch number on a wrapper for? | Finding and recalling every packet from one batch if something is wrong | Showing how many sweets are inside the packet | Giving the phone number of the worker who made it | Showing the price in a secret code
exercise: Why must children never boil sugar without an adult? | Melted sugar is far hotter than boiling water and sticks to skin | Boiling sugar always explodes in the pan | Boiled sugar turns into a poison | The smell of hot sugar makes people faint
exercise: A bag has 5 red, 4 green and 3 yellow jelly bears. How many bears is that? | 12 | 11 | 13 | 9
test: «choc got left in the car, it's gone all grey, bin it?» What is right? | It is safe to eat; the fat just set the wrong way after melting | Bin it, grey chocolate is poisonous | It has gone mouldy in just one hour | Grey means the cocoa inside has burnt to ash in the heat of the car
test: «my sis says chocolate grows on trees as bars lol» What is the true version? | Cocoa beans grow in pods on trees, and factories turn them into bars | Yes, bars hang on cocoa trees ready to pick | Chocolate is dug out of mines deep under the ground and then cut into bars | Chocolate comes from the milk of brown cows
test: In a film, a chocolate factory has a chocolate waterfall. What real step is most like it? | A falling curtain of melted chocolate that coats filled bars | Workers swimming in tanks of chocolate to keep it stirred and smooth | A waterfall that washes the cocoa pods | Rain made of chocolate over the cocoa farms
test: What would happen if melted chocolate were poured into moulds without tempering? | It would set dull and crumbly instead of glossy and snappy | It would never set at all and would stay runny in the mould for ever | It would turn back into milk and sugar | It would set into a gummy sweet
test: «bet u can make lollies in a pan, just boil sugar, easy» What is the best answer? | Never without an adult: melted sugar is hotter than boiling water and sticks to skin | Easy, melted sugar is only warm | Do it alone in the microwave, that's safe | Just dip a finger in to check it's ready
test: A wrapping machine wraps 300 sweets a minute. How many does it wrap in 2 minutes? | 600 | 302 | 500 | 900
test: «ate a gummy bear, it was all dusty, gross, is that mould??» What is most likely? | Cornflour left from the shaping trays | Mould that grew from dirt on the factory floor | Sugar on the outside that has turned into sand | Fluff and dust from inside the bear's wrapper
test: «bloody gum on the pavement again» Why is spat gum a problem for towns? | It sticks hard and costs a lot to clean off | It grows into weeds that push up between the stones | It melts holes in the pavement in hot summer weather | It slowly turns the pavement stones into rubber
test: «it's past the best-before, so it'll make me sick right» What is right? | Not necessarily: best-before is about quality, use-by on fresh food is about safety | Yes, it turns poisonous at midnight that day | Yes, best-before dates are only about safety | No, the dates are only there for decoration
test: A box of chocolates has 3 layers of 8. Mum eats 2. How many are left? | 22 | 24 | 9 | 26
test: «mate's dad says shiny sweets are painted one by one» What is the true way? | Panning: they tumble in drums while thin coats are sprayed on | Each sweet is painted by hand with a brush | They are dipped one by one in nail varnish and left to dry on racks | They are rubbed shiny with a cloth
test: «cocoa farmers must be loaded, choc costs loads» What is right? | Many cocoa farmers earn only a small part of the price of a bar | They get almost all of the money that people pay for each bar of chocolate | Cocoa farmers own the chocolate shops too | Cocoa farmers are paid only in chocolate
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the process known as tempering. | To cause the fat to crystallise in a regular manner, yielding gloss and a clean snap | To render the chocolate entirely free of sugar | To impart a grey colouration to the surface | To remove the fat from the chocolate altogether
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning fresh cocoa beans. | They possess a bitter taste bearing no resemblance to that of chocolate | They possess the taste of milk chocolate | They are ordinarily consumed fresh by the purchaser, much as one eats fruit | They are grown principally in northern Europe
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a boiled sweet? | Sugar syrup from which almost all water has been driven off, forming a sugar glass | A confection into which molten glass has been incorporated | A jelly stiffened by prolonged freezing | A baked product resembling bread
exam: Determine the aggregate quantity of sweets contained in four bags, it being given that each bag contains fifteen sweets. | Sixty | Nineteen | Forty-five | Seventy-five
